Undocumented and unchecked — the caller has to guess what happens on empty:

```java
class Stack<T> {
    T pop() {
        return items.remove(items.size() - 1); // throws IndexOutOfBounds — undocumented
    }
}
```

With the contract made explicit and enforced:

```java
class Stack<T> {
    /**
     * Precondition: !isEmpty()
     * Postcondition: returns the top item; size decreases by 1
     */
    T pop() {
        if (items.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("pop() precondition violated: stack is empty");
        }
        return items.remove(items.size() - 1);
    }
}
```

The check doesn't add new behavior — an empty stack was always going to fail — but it turns a confusing generic exception into a clear statement of exactly which promise the caller broke.
